/bin/sh: -c: line 1: `fi'
" exit 2 / container "sh: -c: line 1: ..." exit 2 (was exit 0, empty); `{
}` names `}' exit 2/2; `( )` names `)' line 0 exit 2/2; `(
)` names `)' line 1 exit 2/2; `for i in 1; do <nl> done` names `done' exit 2/2; `if true; then <nl> fi <nl> echo after` exit 2 with no output (was "after
"); `if false; then echo x; fi <nl> if true; then <nl> fi` names `fi' on line 2. `while true; do <nl> done`, `until false; do <nl> done` and `while true; do <nl> done <nl> echo after` now return exit 2 with "sh: -c: line 1: syntax error near unexpected token `done'" (three cases run in their own container, 150 s wall, no hang). Published 0d8fa78eae57739006a002c20cb5083d294c52b7.

